CacheFn now isolates cache operations by namespace, preserves cached undefined/None values, shares pending fills, and prevents superseded fetches from overwriting explicit writes. Memoization gains explicit TTL/LRU/statistics contracts and type-aware keys. Versioned serialization preserves supported non-JSON values without interpreting ordinary user fields as metadata.

Browser storage includes localStorage/fallback choices, memory-pressure handling, priority clearing and disposal. Offline support provides registered-fetcher refresh, bounded queued updates, persistence, status and FIFO retry. HTTP caching preserves supported response bodies/status/headers across Express, Hono and Fastify, partitions configured variants and bypasses unsafe or authenticated cases. Admin operations use the shared dispatcher for permissions, scope, confirmation and required audit infrastructure.

Priority-filtered clear now fences matching pending fills before awaiting storage, including keys with no stored entry. Nonmatching priorities remain eligible to complete. A superseded task cannot remove a replacement fill or overwrite a later explicit write. This protects fills not yet admitted to storage; it does not establish linearizability for arbitrary concurrent asynchronous adapter writes and metadata/delete operations.

Latest validation: 223 TypeScript tests, typecheck and ESM/CommonJS/declaration builds pass. A fresh consumer installs local CacheFn0.0.3, TestFn0.0.4, shared DB0.4.0 and observability0.0.1 tarballs, plus Vitest4.1.11 and Drizzle0.45.4. SDK/framework resolution is confined to that consumer. Actual TestFn runner execution verifies fixture reuse, concurrent consumers, cleanup after success/failure, and propagation of an intentional failed assertion. npm11.21 installs the graph normally; npm10 reproduces an internal resolver failure. Artifact hashes and reproduction scripts are recorded under .conduct. The old example's lifecycle hooks belong to Vitest; TestFn supplies the runner.

Compatibility: versioned IndexedDB keys intentionally produce a cold cache instead of migrating ambiguous legacy keys. Automatic storage failover does not migrate existing entries. Offline delivery remains at least once and requires server-side idempotency. Direct admin service calls do not replace dispatcher enforcement.

Remaining concrete gaps include unbounded registered-fetcher retention, including disposal cleanup, and the standalone synchronous memoizer's O(number of entries) hit scan. Native Safari and Firefox acceptance, populated-cache performance, broader distributed tiers/full-spec capabilities and deployment gates remain open. No Python runtime changes were made in this increment; Python clear has no priority-filter option. This remains partial production-readiness work.

Targets sf-next, stacked on shared prerequisites #55. Function .conduct evidence is included. No generated artifacts or new ignore rules are included in these increments. No release, deployment or base-branch merge.
EOF
